WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:01.429
Hello there!

00:00:01.429 --> 00:00:04.630
Did you really believe
that “Attention is all you need”?

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:08.140
According to the papers of the last weeks,
not anymore.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:14.280
In today’s video, we showcase the FNet paper
replacing the self-attention (in BERT) with

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.660
Fourier Transforms!

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.140
"Foururorie", what?

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.240
Ms. Coffee Bean, did you know it all along
that self-attention is doomed?

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.940
Then why did'nt you tell me?

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:30.320
Is it because I left you in the coffee roaster
for such a long time?

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:31.320
Here we go:

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:37.530
The FNet, proposed in this paper, does one
modification to the transfomer architecture,

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:41.020
but this one thing has quite the impact.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:46.670
For a bit of context here:
The Transformer -- composed of self-attention,

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:51.550
followed by fully-connected layers and not
to forget about the residual connections and

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:58.829
layer normalization -- had been proven to
be extremely successful on multiple data modalities:

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:05.190
It started with text, became mainstream on
both image and text, then on image only (watch

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:09.580
Ms. Coffee Bean’s videos for all of this),
then it moved on to speech and even further

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:10.590
than that.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.520
But powerful as it is, the Transformer has
a problem:

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:20.740
The self-attention mechanism scales quadratically
in time and memory with increasing sequence

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:21.740
length.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.240
In other words, there is a limit to what the
length of your text might be.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:33.180
For images, the size or resolution also determines
the sequence length and with usual transformers

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.980
you can reach the sequence length limit quite
fast.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:43.640
So far, methods to reduce this quadratically
scaling attention to a linearly scaling operation

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:52.140
have left the concept of attention untouched:
methods like Linformer, Performer, and others,

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:58.110
have cleverly approximated self-attention
matrices, sped up computations and enabled

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:04.440
much longer sequences; but the concept of
attention stayed mostly as it was.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.240
Well, so far...!

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.989
Because now heretic papers are coming out
and rebel against attention, "no, it’s not

00:02:11.989 --> 00:02:13.870
attention that we need anymore!".

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:19.989
Ms. Coffee Bean, is machine learning now a
teenager and doesn't want attention anymore?

00:02:19.989 --> 00:02:25.230
Because this is exactly what FNet also does:
it replaces the self-attention layers through

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:26.840
Fourier Transforms.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:32.500
But the most important question here is:
How dare the authors make such a move and

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:37.769
not call their paper “Fourier is all you
need”?

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:41.129
The audacity of these authors is unbelievable
… Sarcasm ...

00:02:41.129 --> 00:02:46.749
Ok, so let’s see how this replacement of
attention works and what it brings.

00:02:46.749 --> 00:02:53.010
The FNet authors take the classical Transformer
block and modify it by replacing the self-attention

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:58.829
layer containing learnable parameters,
with an unparameterized, thus not a learnt

00:02:58.829 --> 00:03:02.230
transformation, being the Fourier Transform.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:07.879
So before, for each token, self-attention
layers were learning to compute importance

00:03:07.879 --> 00:03:10.700
scores for the token given all other tokens.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:16.670
So to emphasize, the computation of the importance
is learnt from data,

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:23.439
is task-specific and the new representation
for each token was the sum of all tokens weighted

00:03:23.439 --> 00:03:25.979
by their importance to the original token.

00:03:25.979 --> 00:03:33.109
Now with the Fourier Transform, the new representation
for a token is not learnt, but it is a predefined

00:03:33.109 --> 00:03:35.479
mixing scheme of tokens.

00:03:35.479 --> 00:03:40.499
This makes this whole thing faster, because
this layer doesn’t perform any gradient

00:03:40.499 --> 00:03:47.230
updates, but is also unchanged from task to
task or one training scenario to another.

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:52.110
The mixing scheme of the tokens is following
the rules of the Fourier Transformation.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.089
Now, what exactly is this?

00:03:54.089 --> 00:04:00.650
This is where the math gets a little complicated
but it does not need to scare you: Here it

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:06.150
is just written that each token is multiplied
by sines and cosines, because this e to the

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:12.629
power of something, we can write as a superposition
of sines and cosines, handwavy like this.

00:04:12.629 --> 00:04:17.409
So for example the first token is a weighted
sum of all other tokens but here the weights

00:04:17.409 --> 00:04:19.709
are modulated by sines and cosines.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:26.330
But because we are at the first token where
k equals zero, the frequency of the sines

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:32.491
and cosines -- hidden in this exponential
-- is zero, so the first token is just a sum

00:04:32.491 --> 00:04:33.830
of all other tokens.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:39.870
For the second token, the modulation is more
interesting by superimposing waves that increase

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:43.030
in frequency as we add more tokens.

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:48.460
For the third token, the frequency of each
wave we add increases even more, like this.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:55.470
To put it as an analogy, the new representation
of each token is like a cake, where the base

00:04:55.470 --> 00:05:01.600
(frequency) is still the first token, then
the second token adds some detail and becomes

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.340
the creme on the cake,
then the third token becomes the even more

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:10.220
detailed icing,
the fourth token is the super-detailed nuts,

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:16.050
and so on
and then the whole cake is the new token representation,

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:18.900
because we take the summation of all of this.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:23.020
And to be fair, all of this has a real and
a complex components, but forget about it,

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:28.800
because after all the operations are computed,
the authors delete the imaginary component.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:35.490
Anyway, the important messages here are: Firstly,
the Fourier Transform applied to tokens mixes

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.020
the tokens among themselves.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:43.240
It's kind of similar to what self-attention
does but self-attention learned the weights

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:45.950
by which these things need to be mixed.

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:52.330
Also, the same thing is done to the hidden
dimensions, so that the features of the tokens

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:54.620
are also mixed among each other.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:59.680
And until here the transformation that was
done is reversible by the inverse Fourier

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:00.680
Transform.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:03.860
In other words,
the components of the cake can be recovered

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.789
by decomposing after each frequency.

00:06:06.789 --> 00:06:14.020
It is the next step, the feed-forward layers
that mix things even more and usually not

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:16.330
in an invertible way.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:22.650
The second important message is that the mixing
scheme or pattern is not learnt, it is fixed

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:28.580
and always follows the same rule, making the
architecture simpler and easier to compute.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:34.090
It decreases the number of the learnable parameters
of the Transfomer which now is technically

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:35.590
FNet and not the Transformer...

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:40.840
Thirdly, a more advanced take home message
also mentioned in the paper, which Ms.Coffee

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:46.819
Bean found super-interesting, is the following:
You maybe know, or just take Ms. Coffee Bean’s

00:06:46.819 --> 00:06:53.610
word for it, convolutions in the real/time
domain correspond to simple multiplications

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:55.960
in the Fourier/frequency domain.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:07:02.699
So the first Fourier layer of the architecture
moves the representation from the real domain

00:07:02.699 --> 00:07:09.340
to the Fourier domain and multiplications
in these layers happening afterwards, are

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:13.120
now actually convolutions in the real space.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:16.419
So by adding
more and more Transformer layers, each one

00:07:16.419 --> 00:07:22.830
with one Fourier layer, FNet is jumping back
and forth from multiplications to convolutions.

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:28.310
In other words, convolutions are somehow here
and perhaps now are taking revenge on the

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:31.469
Transformers and crash their party!

00:07:31.469 --> 00:07:33.000
Are convolutions all you need??

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:38.229
I actually cannot hear “all you need”
anymore, but Ms. Coffee Bean makes me say

00:07:38.229 --> 00:07:39.229
it..

00:07:39.229 --> 00:07:43.340
Ok but one question remains: Why exactly the
Fourier Transform, you might wonder?

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:48.319
Reading the paper, it becomes clear that the
first thing the authors tried was simple linear

00:07:48.319 --> 00:07:51.970
mixing schemes,
but then went for something more structured

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:53.639
like the Fourier Transform.

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:59.509
But believe it or not, the other paper getting
rid of attention, the MLP-Mixer

00:07:59.509 --> 00:08:03.009
learns mixing patterns that look very much
like…

00:08:03.009 --> 00:08:04.009
Fourier!

00:08:04.009 --> 00:08:09.400
Just look, here is the base frequency, here
is the double of that frequency with its negative

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:10.669
counterpart.

00:08:10.669 --> 00:08:16.009
The MLP-Mixer, found something like the Fourier
scheme on itself, but on images!

00:08:16.009 --> 00:08:20.990
FNet has been applied to text and has the
Fourier transformation hard-coded and it seems

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:22.349
to work also well.

00:08:22.349 --> 00:08:28.569
So, the FNet basically is a BERT variant without
attention but Fourier Transformations and

00:08:28.569 --> 00:08:34.529
achieves 92% of the accuracy of BERT on GLUE
(an important NLU benchmark), which is not

00:08:34.529 --> 00:08:38.330
exactly 100% of BERT, but good enough.

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:44.000
And especially because there is no quadratically
scaling attention anymore, it runs seven times

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:50.370
faster on GPU and two times faster on TPUs
and you can increase the sequence length!

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:55.680
So self-attention was praised as being the
recipe to success for quite a while, but now

00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:02.389
papers like FNet, MLP-Mixer and gMLP relativize
this by a bit, showing that if there is enough

00:09:02.389 --> 00:09:06.470
data and compute, the architecture does not
matter much.

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:12.820
So it seems like back in the days when MLPs
were a thing, we discarded MLPs too early

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:18.150
when perhaps the only thing that was missing
then was the data and compute scale we have

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:24.130
now, so we preferred CNNs or RNNs because
of their inductive biases (always a good idea

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:25.380
with little data).

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:32.730
But to say this is very unfair because architectures
like FNet or MLP-Mixer are not exactly like

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:39.370
a vanilla MLP from back then; the structure
of these MLPs follows the trend set by Transformers,

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:45.750
so Ms. Coffee Bean sees this as an evolution
and not really as a backwards... involution

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:46.750
or something.

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:51.970
In any case, now that we have seen that long
sequences are not such of a problem anymore

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:58.279
and that image token mixing and text token
mixing looks quite similar, Ms. Coffee Bean

00:09:58.279 --> 00:10:04.390
thinks it's great news for multimodality where
the goal is to combine many types of input

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:08.490
data, that potentially comes in very long
sequences.

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:13.699
Especially if you append one modality to another,
like Vision &amp; Language Transformers do.

00:10:13.699 --> 00:10:17.769
The FNet paper is very well-written, so what
are you waiting for?

00:10:17.769 --> 00:10:23.529
Leave a like, subscribe, share Ms. Coffee
Bean to send her around the world and go read

00:10:23.529 --> 00:10:25.029
the paper yourself!

00:10:25.029 --> 00:10:26.950
Okay bye!

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:32.529
Ms. Coffee Bean, it has been a while since
your last video, are you getting lazy or what?

00:10:32.529 --> 00:10:33.529
Oh...!

00:10:33.529 --> 00:10:39.500
Yes, I know I put you in the coffee roaster
and left you there, but I was busy with moving

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:41.630
and with a paper.

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:42.860
No, no!

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:44.709
I did not waste my time.

00:10:44.709 --> 00:10:47.839
No, Ms. Coffee Bean that’s rude to say!

00:10:47.839 --> 00:10:49.060
Go to your coffee roaster!

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:50.139
Okay bye!

